  <h1>Low-Latency vs Ultra-Low-Latency</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: across Lessons 0001/0002/0004/0009/0010
    you've seen the same arithmetic from microsecond to nanosecond viewpoints. This
    lesson formalizes the terminology — what counts as "low-latency" vs "ultra-low-latency"
    — and gives you the decision framework for which tier a strategy needs to live at.
    Once you have the ladder, the techniques that move you down it become straightforward.
  </p>

  <p class="illus">
    Note: per the new repo guidance, code blocks in this lesson are <em>illustrative</em>,
    not always from the repo. We'll backfill into real code as we build it out.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 DEFINITIONS ===================== -->
  <h2>1. Definitions — there's no formal standard, but a strong industry consensus</h2>

  <p>
    "Low-latency" and "ultra-low-latency" are <strong>marketing terms</strong> with no
    formal standards body to bless them. But the trading industry has settled on
    rough conventions that map cleanly onto the technology tiers:
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>Typical tick-to-trade range</th><th>What the system looks like</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>"Fast" / standard</strong></td>
      <td>milliseconds (1 ms+)</td>
      <td>Default Python or untuned C++ over kernel TCP. Your FastAPI webapp.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Low-latency (LL)</strong></td>
      <td>microseconds (1–100 µs)</td>
      <td>Tuned C++ on Linux with isolated cores, lock-free queues, occasional kernel bypass. This is Lessons 0009–0010 territory.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Ultra-low-latency (ULL)</strong></td>
      <td>nanoseconds (10 ns – 1 µs)</td>
      <td>FPGA tick-to-trade, kernel-bypass NICs, colocation, microwave/RF links across cities, hardware timestamping.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The most useful single discriminator:</strong> if your hot path crosses the
    Linux kernel even once, you're in LL territory at best. If your hot path lives
    entirely on the FPGA or in user-space against a kernel-bypass NIC, you're
    in ULL territory. The kernel boundary is the rough dividing line.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 THE LADDER ===================== -->
  <h2>2. The latency ladder, end-to-end</h2>

  <p>
    Each rung swaps a technique for a faster one. Tick-to-trade numbers are
    representative — they're a moving target as silicon and tooling improve, but
    the <em>ratios</em> between rungs are stable.
  </p>

  <div class="ladder">
    <div class="rung tier-0">
      <div class="tier">Tier 0 — Web app</div>
      <div class="desc">Python/Node over public internet. Your FastAPI dev server.</div>
      <div class="t2t">~50 ms</div>
      <div class="cost">$10/mo</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rung tier-0">
      <div class="tier">Tier 1 — Retail</div>
      <div class="desc">Tuned Python or untuned C++ over kernel TCP from a co-region cloud instance.</div>
      <div class="t2t">~1–5 ms</div>
      <div class="cost">$100/mo</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rung tier-1">
      <div class="tier">Tier 2 — LL CPU</div>
      <div class="desc">Tuned C++ + lock-free queues + isolated cores (Lesson 0010). Kernel TCP.</div>
      <div class="t2t">~50–200 µs</div>
      <div class="cost">~$1k/mo</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rung tier-2">
      <div class="tier">Tier 3 — Kernel bypass</div>
      <div class="desc">DPDK or Solarflare OpenOnload — user-space drives the NIC directly.</div>
      <div class="t2t">~750 ns – 5 µs</div>
      <div class="cost">~$5k/mo</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rung tier-3">
      <div class="tier">Tier 4 — FPGA tick-to-trade</div>
      <div class="desc">Strategy logic synthesized into HDL on the NIC (the repo's <code>fpga/</code> direction).</div>
      <div class="t2t">~100–300 ns</div>
      <div class="cost">~$50k/mo</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rung tier-4">
      <div class="tier">Tier 5 — Hardware MAC pipeline</div>
      <div class="desc">Parsing happens inside the PHY/MAC itself; multiple parallel decoders.</div>
      <div class="t2t">~20–100 ns</div>
      <div class="cost">~$100k+/mo</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rung tier-5">
      <div class="tier">Tier 6 — Custom ASIC + microwave</div>
      <div class="desc">Tape-out silicon for the matching logic; line-of-sight microwave between data centers.</div>
      <div class="t2t">~10 ns + RF</div>
      <div class="cost">$M/year</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Two things worth noticing about this ladder:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The jump from Tier 2 (LL) to Tier 3 (ULL) is <strong>~100×</strong> latency.
      Below Tier 3 every further rung is ~3–10×. The big cliff is the kernel boundary.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost is roughly geometric.</strong> Each rung is order-of-magnitude
      more expensive than the one above. ULL is a financial decision, not just an
      engineering one.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ===================== §3 SIX TECHNIQUES ===================== -->
  <h2>3. The six techniques that buy you ULL</h2>

  <p>Roughly in the order they pay off:</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>1. Colocation</h4>
      <p>
        Rent rack space inside the exchange's data center so your wire to the
        matching engine is meters, not kilometers. Cuts ~100 µs of fiber latency
        for free (speed of light is 5 µs/km in glass). Step zero before any of the
        below makes sense.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>2. Kernel bypass (DPDK, OpenOnload)</h4>
      <p>
        Route packets from NIC DMA rings directly into your user-space process,
        skipping the kernel TCP stack (Lesson 0004). Saves ~50 µs per round-trip.
        Mandatory entry to ULL territory on CPU.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>3. FPGA tick-to-trade</h4>
      <p>
        Synthesize the strategy into HDL on the NIC itself (Lesson 0002).
        Parsing → decision → order all happen on-chip in nanoseconds.
        The biggest single jump in the ladder.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>4. CPU/kernel discipline</h4>
      <p>
        <code>isolcpus</code>, <code>nohz_full</code>, busy polling,
        thread pinning, mlockall (Lesson 0010). Cuts jitter — the tail of the
        latency distribution — to near zero on the CPU paths that remain.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>5. Cross-market microwave / RF links</h4>
      <p>
        For arbitrage between geographically separated venues, line-of-sight
        microwave links beat fiber. Light through air ≈ 1.0c; through glass ≈
        0.67c. Chicago↔New Jersey: ~8 ms one-way over microwave vs ~13 ms over
        fiber — a 5 ms edge that funded an entire industry of tower-builders in
        the 2010s.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>6. Custom silicon (ASIC)</h4>
      <p>
        At the top of the ladder, the biggest firms tape out application-specific
        chips that hard-code parsing, decision, and risk-check in fewer cycles
        than an FPGA can. Multi-million-dollar capex; only the largest desks
        justify it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>A worked example — measuring where the time goes</h3>

  <p>
    A hypothetical tick-to-trade breakdown at each tier, so you can see what each
    technique removes:
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Tier 2 (LL CPU)</th><th>Tier 3 (Kernel bypass)</th><th>Tier 4 (FPGA)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Packet on wire → host</td><td>~2 µs</td><td>~2 µs</td><td>~25 ns</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Kernel network stack</td><td>~50 µs</td><td><em>skipped</em></td><td><em>skipped</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td>User-space parse</td><td>~1 µs</td><td>~1 µs</td><td>~25 ns (on-chip)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Strategy decision</td><td>~500 ns</td><td>~500 ns</td><td>~10 ns</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Order encode + TX</td><td>~5 µs</td><td>~1 µs</td><td>~50 ns</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Total tick-to-trade</th><th>~60 µs</th><th>~5 µs</th><th>~110 ns</th></tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    Notice that once kernel and userland are skipped (Tier 4), <em>the parse and
    decision stages dominate again</em> — and shaving them is exactly what
    <code>fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> and
    <code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code> in this repo do, in nanoseconds.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §4 MEASURING HONESTLY ===================== -->
  <h2>4. How you actually measure latency — and the numbers that lie</h2>

  <p>
    "Our system is 5 µs" is the wrong sentence. The right sentence has a
    <em>distribution</em>, an <em>endpoint pair</em>, and a <em>clock source</em>.
  </p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Distribution, not average</h4>
      <p>
        The mean is almost meaningless. HFT lives on the tail. Quote
        <strong>p50 / p99 / p99.9 / p99.99</strong> — the latencies that 50%,
        99%, etc. of events come in under. A 5 µs mean with a 500 µs p99.9 will
        lose money even on a fast median; the tail is the killer.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Endpoint pair (be precise)</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Wire-to-wire</strong>: last bit of incoming packet → first bit of
        outgoing order at the NIC. <strong>App-to-app</strong>: feature extracted →
        decision made (your code only). <strong>Inside-the-FPGA</strong>: single-cycle
        registered output. State which one or comparisons mean nothing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Clock source</h4>
      <p>
        <code>clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)</code> has 10–30 ns resolution but
        burns a syscall and can step. <code>rdtsc</code> reads the TSC register
        in ~10 ns but needs <code>tsc_reliable</code> and per-core sync.
        <strong>Hardware timestamps</strong> (NIC or FPGA-side) are the only
        truth at ULL — they have ns resolution and are immune to OS noise.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Jitter, not just latency</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Jitter</strong> = the variability of latency, often quoted as
        <em>p99.99 − p50</em> or as the standard deviation. ULL systems live
        on low jitter; a deterministic 5 µs beats an "average 1 µs" with a
        wild tail every time, because risk models price the worst case.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>A minimum-viable measurement snippet (illustrative):</p>
<pre><span class="kw">struct</span> Sample { <span class="ty">uint64_t</span> t_in_ns; <span class="ty">uint64_t</span> t_out_ns; };
std::<span class="ty">array</span>&lt;Sample, <span class="num">1</span> &lt;&lt; <span class="num">20</span>&gt; samples;   <span class="com">// 1M-sample ring of timing pairs</span>

<span class="kw">inline</span> <span class="ty">uint64_t</span> <span class="fn">now_ns</span>() <span class="kw">noexcept</span> {
    timespec ts; <span class="fn">clock_gettime</span>(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW, &amp;ts);
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="ty">uint64_t</span>(ts.tv_sec) * <span class="num">1'000'000'000ull</span> + ts.tv_nsec;
}

<span class="com">// Per tick: record entry, do work, record exit, store the pair, never log.</span>
samples[i].t_in_ns  = <span class="fn">now_ns</span>();
strategy.<span class="fn">onTick</span>(tick);
samples[i].t_out_ns = <span class="fn">now_ns</span>();
</pre>

  <p>
    Offline (cold path), sort the deltas and compute the quantiles. Plot the
    histogram. The story is always in the tail.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §5 COST CLIFF ===================== -->
  <h2>5. The cost cliff — and how to decide what tier you actually need</h2>

  <p>
    Each rung of the ladder is ~10× more expensive than the one above. The
    decision of which tier to target is an economic argument, not a technical
    one. Ask three questions before committing:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>What's your competition's tier?</strong>
      If everyone on your venue is at FPGA tick-to-trade and you're at CPU,
      your fills will be adversely selected (you trade only when faster traders
      didn't want to) and your P&L is structurally negative. Conversely, if
      everyone is at LL CPU, paying for FPGA buys you nothing.
    </li>
    <li><strong>What's your strategy's natural cadence?</strong>
      A daily-rebalance equity model (this repo's GBM) is at Tier 0–1 by design;
      moving it to Tier 4 is malpractice. A market-making strategy on a liquid
      futures contract starts to need Tier 3 to be viable and Tier 4 to be
      competitive.
    </li>
    <li><strong>What's the marginal P&L of one less microsecond?</strong>
      In a market-making strategy, less latency means winning more queue
      positions, fewer adverse fills, and a bigger fraction of the spread captured.
      For a directional next-day model: zero marginal P&L from sub-µs latency.
      Drawing this curve is what justifies the spend.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>The single most common HFT-firm misallocation:</strong> a junior
    engineer rebuilds a Tier 1 strategy as a Tier 4 system on the assumption
    that "faster is better." It's not. Faster is more expensive, harder to
    debug, and only pays off when the strategy itself is latency-bound.
    Profile first. Tier the strategy intentionally.
  </div>

