  <!-- ===================== §6 OFTEN CONFUSED ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Latency vs Jitter</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Latency</strong>: how long one event takes (p50, p99…).
        <strong>Jitter</strong>: how variable that is (p99.99 − p50, or stddev).
        ULL systems often sacrifice average latency for lower jitter; risk
        models care about the worst case.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Latency vs Throughput</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Latency</strong>: time per event. <strong>Throughput</strong>:
        events per second. HFT optimizes for latency; market-data plants
        optimize for throughput. They often trade off — busy polling cuts
        latency by burning a core, lowering throughput per watt.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>p50 vs p99 vs p99.99</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>p50</strong> (median): what the typical event experiences.
        <strong>p99</strong>: 1-in-100 events are slower. <strong>p99.99</strong>:
        1-in-10,000 events are slower. At market-data rates of 100k+
        messages/sec, p99.99 events happen several times a second.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Round-trip vs One-way</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Round-trip</strong> tick-to-trade includes the order leaving
        your NIC. <strong>One-way</strong> sometimes refers to wire-to-wire
        latency or to inbound parsing only. Most published "tick-to-trade"
        numbers are wire-to-wire round-trip; always confirm.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>LL vs ULL vs Real-time</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Real-time</strong> in OS terms means "bounded latency" not
        "fast." A 10 ms deterministic system is real-time; a 100 ns average
        with a 10 ms tail is not. ULL systems aim to be real-time first and
        fast second.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Speed vs Edge</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Speed</strong> is a capability (your tier). <strong>Edge</strong>
        is the advantage that capability gives you on a particular strategy.
        Speed without edge is wasted spend; speed with edge is alpha.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

