  <!-- ===================== §8 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>8. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. Your strategy team reports a Sharpe of 2.0 in backtest on a Tier 2 system, but Sharpe of −0.3 when live. The latency was identical to backtest. What's the most likely cause and how does the tier conversation help?</summary>
    <p>
      Adverse selection. In backtest, you "fill" every order at the recorded price.
      Live, faster participants see the same signal microseconds before you and
      lift the offers you wanted — leaving you with only the bad fills (the ones
      no one else wanted, where the price was about to move against you). The
      backtest assumed you were the only player; the market is full of Tier 4
      players. Tier conversation: either accept that this strategy needs Tier 3+
      to be viable, or pick a different strategy whose alpha doesn't depend on
      being faster than other participants — e.g. multi-day signals where the
      first-fill advantage is irrelevant.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. You measure p50 = 700 ns and p99.99 = 3 µs on a kernel-bypass system. A new release ships and p50 drops to 500 ns, but p99.99 jumps to 25 µs. Should you ship the new release?</summary>
    <p>
      No — and the question is itself a teaching moment about what to optimize.
      A 200 ns mean win is mathematically beaten by a 22 µs tail blow-up because
      the tail events happen thousands of times per day at typical market-data
      rates, and each one likely loses money on adverse selection. The new
      release also implies you've introduced some jitter source (a new
      allocation, a new branch on a rare path, a cache eviction pattern, a
      removed busy-poll). Diagnose what's causing the tail before shipping;
      revert if necessary. The ULL maxim: <em>median is vanity, tail is sanity</em>.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. The repo's <code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code> targets ~4 ns per stage. If you wrap it in a Linux user-space C++ harness that polls the FPGA over PCIe for every quote, what tier does the combined system actually live at, and why?</summary>
    <p>
      Whatever the slowest link is. PCIe Gen3 round-trip from a CPU to a card
      sitting in a PCIe slot is typically 500 ns – 2 µs, dominated by the
      driver, the user-space polling pattern, and the bus itself. So even
      though the FPGA does its arithmetic in ~4 ns, a CPU-in-the-loop system
      operates at Tier 3 (kernel-bypass-ish, ~µs) — not Tier 4. To live at
      Tier 4 the orders must <em>leave the FPGA directly</em> over the SFP+
      port, with the CPU only used as a slow-path configurator. That's why
      the README's roadmap groups "10 GbE UDP stack" and "PCIe DMA interface"
      as separate items: one is the ULL path, the other is the slow-path
      management interface.
    </p>
  </details>

